1. CRITICAL RULE — CHARACTER IDENTITY MUST NOT CHANGE BASELINE MOBILITY

This is an absolute design rule.

Every playable character must have exactly the same baseline movement capabilities.

Character lore, appearance, archetype, name or identity must never secretly alter normal movement statistics.

For example:

#0002 may be described as a “speedster,” but that does NOT mean #0002 runs faster, accelerates faster, jumps higher, Dashes faster or gets superior air mobility.

The word “speedster” can describe the character’s fantasy, animations and unique attacks such as Homing Attack, Spin Attack, Bounce Attack, etc.

It must not grant superior universal locomotion.

Likewise, no future character should become slower because they look heavy or stronger because they look muscular.

Universal baseline

All playable characters must share the same values/rules for normal:

* maximum run speed;
* ground acceleration;
* ground deceleration;
* turn acceleration / turn boost;
* overspeed handling;
* air acceleration;
* air deceleration;
* air turning;
* normal jump velocity / jump height;
* higher-jump behavior;
* air-jump count;
* air-jump strength;
* gravity scale;
* maximum normal fall speed;
* fast-fall acceleration;
* fast-fall speed;
* coyote time;
* jump buffer;
* combat input buffer where it relates to universal responsiveness;
* Dash speed;
* Dash input window;
* air-dash speed;
* air-dash uses;
* other generic mobility statistics introduced by this update.

A player selecting another fighter should not need to relearn basic movement.

Current inequality that must be removed

The repository currently intentionally gives the fighters different movement profiles.

Among other differences:

* #0001 uses Speed Power 2.
* #0002 uses Speed Power 3.
* #0001 acceleration is approximately 4200.
* #0002 acceleration is approximately 4800.
* #0001 Dash / air dash is approximately 950 units/s.
* #0002 Dash / air dash is approximately 1100 units/s.

That distinction is no longer desired.

Normalize it.

The existing documentation even uses “#0002 is faster” as an example of character configuration. That design philosophy must be updated.

Architecture

Prefer a single shared baseline movement configuration rather than copying identical numbers into every fighter file.

Conceptually, something such as:

export const BASE_FIGHTER_MOVEMENT = Object.freeze({
  ...
});

or another clean shared architecture is preferable.

Character definitions may still describe unique move mechanics, but they should not own arbitrary variations in universal locomotion.

A newly-added fighter should automatically inherit the same baseline without its author needing to manually copy 15 movement numbers.

Do not special-case IDs:

if (fighter.def.id === '0001') ...
if (fighter.def.id === '0002') ...

is unacceptable.

