6. MOMENTUM IS A CORE DESIGN PILLAR

This is one of the most important requirements of the update:

A legal action transition should almost never destroy existing momentum.

Audit every place where vx or vy is:

* zeroed;
* replaced;
* clamped;
* reduced;
* reset;
* normalized to normal top speed;
* silently discarded by a state transition.

Remove arbitrary momentum loss.

Current problem: attackStartSpeed

The current shared movement code intentionally clamps a grounded attack’s inherited velocity against normal maxSpeed.

That means high Dash momentum can be thrown away when entering an attack.

This conflicts directly with the new design.

Refactor attackStartSpeed() and the surrounding attack-movement semantics.

A fighter going:

Dash at 1250
→ attack

should not suddenly fall to ordinary running speed just because an attack began.

The attack should inherit the incoming momentum unless the attack’s actual mechanical behavior explicitly redirects it.

Momentum through actions

Preserve horizontal momentum through:

* jump;
* air jump;
* attack startup;
* attack active frames;
* attack recovery;
* hit-cancel;
* Dash cancel;
* Dash → attack;
* Dash → jump;
* air dash → attack;
* air dash → jump where legal;
* landing;
* landing → attack;
* landing → Dash;
* transitions between ordinary locomotion animations;
* Deflect where its mechanics do not explicitly redirect motion.

There should be no hidden state transition that simply assigns:

vx = 0;

because entering that state is convenient.

Dash behavior

Starting a Dash should never make an already-faster fighter slower.

Conceptually:

newSpeed = Math.max(existingForwardSpeed, dashSpeed)

rather than blindly overwriting a faster existing forward velocity with a lower constant.

The exact implementation should respect facing/direction and remain deterministic.

When a Dash finishes, the fighter should carry its velocity into the next state.

Do not end a Dash by resetting to normal run speed.

Overspeed

Normal top speed is the speed locomotion builds toward from rest.

It should not behave as an invisible hard velocity cap on momentum earned from:

* Dash;
* air dash;
* attack motion;
* rebounds;
* another legitimate movement mechanic.

Overspeed may decay smoothly where appropriate, but it must not disappear instantly.

Holding the current travel direction should preserve momentum especially well.

Releasing the stick can gradually brake.

Holding the opposite direction can brake aggressively and reverse.

The difference between those situations should be obvious.

Legitimate exceptions

Actual outside forces may legitimately change momentum:

* being hit;
* a launch;
* a carry;
* a pull;
* a rebound;
* a wall/terrain collision;
* a move whose explicit mechanic is to redirect the user’s body;
* falling into the Void / respawning.

Those are physical/gameplay events.

A state-machine transition is not.

