14. ATTACK MOVEMENT SHOULD FEEL ALIVE

Most normal attacks should not feel as if the fighter’s feet were welded to the floor.

Review existing lockMovement, momentum, airMomentum, control, airControl, friction and step behavior.

Keep these generic systems, but tune ordinary attacks toward better flow.

Where appropriate:

* preserve full incoming momentum;
* allow a useful amount of steering;
* let running attacks slide naturally;
* let aerial attacks retain aerial drift;
* avoid automatically braking characters merely because an attack is playing.

An attack specifically designed to be planted may still be planted.

But “planted” must be an intentional move property, not the default sensation of the entire combat system.

Unique attack motions such as homing, bounce, rise, roll and hover continue to own movement according to their mechanic.

Even there, consider incoming momentum when entering/exiting the mechanic so transitions do not feel discontinuous.

